# Extending {{ project_slug }}

This harness is built to be edited. The three most common changes never require
touching `loop.py`.

## Add a tool

Write a function and register it with the `@registry.tool(...)` decorator in
`src/{{ project_slug }}/harness/tools.py` (or your own module that imports the
shared `registry`):

```python
from {{ project_slug }}.harness.tools import registry

@registry.tool(
    name="word_count",
    description="Count the words in a piece of text.",
    parameters={
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {"text": {"type": "string"}},
        "required": ["text"],
    },
)
def word_count(text: str) -> str:
    return str(len(text.split()))
```

Then allowlist it in `config.yaml` so the model is offered it:

```yaml
tools:
  - name: word_count
    enabled: true
```

A tool is only exposed to the model when it is **enabled in `config.yaml`** — the
allowlist. Keep dangerous tools disabled until you explicitly opt in.

**Risk classification (matters for read-only modes).** A tool's `risk` defaults to
`"safe"`, which also exposes it to the read-only `plan` / `ask` paradigms. So if a
tool of yours **mutates anything** (writes files, runs commands, calls a
state-changing API), mark it `risk="high"`:

```python
@registry.tool(name="write_file", description="...", risk="high")  # agent-only
def write_file(path: str, content: str) -> str: ...
```

Otherwise `plan` / `ask`, which a user trusts to be read-only, can call it. (MCP
tools are the opposite — they default to `risk="high"`, and you opt specific
read-only ones into a server's `safe_tools`.)

Treat `plan` / `ask` read-only as a **model-A guardrail**, not a security sandbox:
high-risk tools aren't offered, anything outside the offered set is refused at
execution, and the prompt tells the model not to change things — but the guarantee
is only as correct as your `risk` labels (a mutating tool you forgot to mark
`high` slips through). For a stronger gate, turn on HITL tool confirmation (the
`confirm` knob — see below) so a flagged tool must be allowed before it runs; for a
hard guarantee against a misbehaving model, run in an isolated environment
(container / read-only filesystem).
{% if interfaces.web %}
Once allowlisted it also appears in the web **Config → Tools** tab, listed under
**Your tools** at the top (the generated built-ins follow; each MCP server's
tools fold into a collapsed group below). The tab is a toggle view of the
allowlist — it doesn't auto-discover a tool you registered but haven't added to
`config.yaml`.
{% endif %}

## Ask the user (ask_question + the interaction channel)

The model can pause the loop and ask the **end user** a clarifying question with
the built-in `ask_question` tool (on by default — `risk="safe"`). It's the right
move when a request is ambiguous or a decision is genuinely the user's (which
option, confirm an assumption). The user's answer comes back as the tool result;
the model continues from there.

- The model can offer `options` (a multiple-choice question) and set `allow_multiple`
  for multi-select. The user can **always** type a custom answer: when options are
  offered, a Cursor-style **"Other…"** entry is added automatically as the last
  choice (picking it reveals a text box), so don't add your own "Other" option.
- **CLI**: the terminal prints the question + numbered options (plus an "Other"
  entry) and reads a line; commas pick several when `allow_multiple`.
- **Web**: an inline card appears in the chat (radios / checkboxes + the "Other…"
  text box); the answer is POSTed to `/chat/{run_id}/respond`, unblocking the loop.
- **Non-interactive** (a pipe, CI, `--mock`, a disconnected web client): there's no
  user, so it degrades — the answer is a "no interactive user available, use your
  best judgment" note and the run never hangs.

Turn it off by removing `ask_question` from `config.yaml`'s `tools:` (or setting
`enabled: false`); nothing else changes.

The plumbing is `harness/interaction.py` — a thin "pause and ask the user" channel
next to `tools` / `hooks`, **not** part of the core loop. An asker is injected per
turn via a `contextvars` ContextVar (`using_asker`), so any tool body can reach the
user without changing a tool signature or a `run()` parameter:

```python
from {{ project_slug }}.harness.interaction import AskRequest, Option, ask

answer = ask(AskRequest(
    kind="question", prompt="Which environment?",
    options=[Option("0", "staging"), Option("1", "prod")], allow_text=True,
))
# answer.selected -> "0"/"1"; answer.text -> a typed reply (when allowed)
```

## Confirm before a tool runs (HITL, opt-in)

The same channel powers **human-in-the-loop tool confirmation**: before the loop
runs a tool you flagged, it pauses and asks you to allow or reject it (`kind=
"approval"` — same pause → prompt → wait → resume round-trip). Off by default, so
the golden path is untouched. Turn it on with the `confirm` knob in `config.yaml`:

```yaml
confirm: high            # none (default) | high | all | [list, of, tool, names]
```

- `none` confirms nothing; `high` confirms `risk="high"` tools (shell / file-writes
  / mutating MCP tools); `all` confirms every tool (read-only included); a list
  confirms exactly the named tools. The trigger is an **allowlist** (is this tool in
  the confirm set?), not a per-call risk guess — only read-only is exempt by default.
- On a prompt you pick one of four (aligned with Codex CLI): **allow once** (run it,
  ask again next time), **reject** (don't run it — the model gets an `ERROR` it can
  adapt to, the loop never crashes), **allow for this session** (stop asking for that
  tool this conversation, in memory), or **always allow** (also writes the tool out
  of the `confirm` policy in `config.yaml`, so it sticks across restarts).
- **CLI**: a numbered prompt in the terminal. **Web**: an inline approval card
  (tool + arguments + a risk badge + the four buttons).
- **Non-interactive** (a pipe / CI / `--mock`) and a stopped or disconnected web run
  **reject by default** (fail-closed).

This is a **guardrail** (it stops a trusted user / model fat-fingering a dangerous
tool), **not a security boundary**: code you own can bypass it, and a capability you
truly want unavailable should not be compiled in at all (don't allowlist it). For a
hard guarantee against a misbehaving model, run in a container. Confirmation lives at
the **tool-execution boundary** (`run_tool` → `interaction.confirm_tool`), not in a
hook, so `hooks.before_tool` stays a pure observer.

## Swap the model / provider

Everything is OpenAI Chat Completions over a `base_url`, so any compatible
endpoint works (OpenAI, vLLM, together, groq, a local server, ...). Edit the
profile in `config.yaml`:

```yaml
llms:
  - name: default
    model: your-model-name
    api_key_env: YOUR_API_KEY        # the NAME of the env-var, not the value
    base_url_env: YOUR_BASE_URL      # optional; omit for api.openai.com
```

Add the matching name(s) to `.env`. To track spend, set
`input_cost_per_million` / `output_cost_per_million` on the profile (price per
1M tokens, in your own currency); `cached_input_cost_per_million` prices
provider-cached prompt tokens (cheaper) when your provider reports them.

Sampling and reliability knobs are optional and sent **only when set** (unset =
the provider's default): `temperature`, `top_p`, `frequency_penalty`,
`presence_penalty`, `seed`, `stop`, and `reasoning_effort` (reasoning models).
For a non-standard provider param with no field of its own — e.g. Qwen/DashScope
`top_k` / `enable_thinking` — use `extra_body`, a dict merged verbatim into the
request (values only, never secrets). `context_window` (the model's total window)
turns on accurate `window_pct` compaction (see "Tune the context window").
`timeout_seconds` / `max_retries` are passed to the SDK; `fallback` names another
profile to retry a step with if a call errors before any output streamed.

```yaml
llms:
  - name: default
    model: your-model-name
    api_key_env: YOUR_API_KEY
    context_window: 200000      # enables window_pct compaction; unset = no early compaction
    fallback: backup            # optional failover to another profile on error
    # top_p: 0.95               # extra_body: {top_k: 40}   # for non-standard params
```

A profile with `provider: anthropic` speaks the **native Anthropic Messages
API** instead (`harness/llm_anthropic.py`) — first-class adaptive thinking
(`reasoning_effort` maps to the `effort` level) and native tool semantics.
Everything else (roles, routing, budget, trace) is identical; the loop never
knows which spec a profile speaks.

**Reasoning passback (thinking models + tools).** When a thinking model reasons
before calling a tool, that reasoning is captured and echoed back on later turns
attached to the tool-calling assistant message — reasoning endpoints (MiMo /
DeepSeek thinking / Qwen / Kimi) **400 the next request if it is missing**, and
native Anthropic requires the signed thinking block to round-trip. It is handled
automatically and only for tool-calling turns, so a model that emits no reasoning
is unaffected. The OpenAI-compatible field defaults to `reasoning_content`; set a
profile's `reasoning_history_field` to `reasoning` for vLLM builds that want that
name, or to `""` to disable passback for an endpoint that rejects the field.

## Project rules (always-apply)

Steer the agent on **every** turn with markdown rule files — the open
`AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` / `.cursor/rules` pattern. Each file listed in
`config.yaml` → `prompts.rules_files` is read and injected into the system
prompt verbatim (a missing file is skipped, so it's never an error):

```yaml
prompts:
  system: |-                # the base prompt (a thin, general default ships here)
    You are a capable AI assistant. Use the available tools when they help, and
    be honest and concise.
  rules_files:
    - RULES.md            # ships as a starter; edit it freely
    - .cursor/rules.md    # or reuse rules you already keep for other tools
```

A **`CLAUDE.md` at the project root is auto-recognized** — dropped in, it's
injected even without listing it (the open convention). This file (`AGENTS.md`)
is the exception: it's the harness's "how to extend me" guide, **not** a runtime
rule, so it is never auto-injected (list it explicitly if you ever want it to be).

This is a **runtime knob** — add/edit rule files and the list without
regenerating. Keep rules short and imperative; every line is sent on every turn,
so each costs tokens. (Unlike a Skill, which is loaded on demand only when
relevant, a rule is *always* in context.)
{% if interfaces.web %}
You can also edit these files in the web **Config → Prompts** tab: each rule file
shows an editable content box (add/remove files there too), and Save writes the
files plus the active list. Paths must stay inside the project directory.
{% endif %}

## Plug in a hook

Subclass `Hooks` to add logging, guardrails, redaction, or human-in-the-loop
confirmation — without changing the loop:

```python
# src/{{ project_slug }}/harness/hooks.py (or your own module)
from {{ project_slug }}.harness.hooks import Hooks

class AuditHooks(Hooks):
    def before_tool(self, call):
        print(f"about to call {call.name} with {call.arguments}")
```

Hook points: `before_step`, `after_step`, `before_tool`, `after_tool`,
`on_error`.

**Mount it without editing any interface code** — point `config.yaml` at the
class by dotted path; the CLI (`run` / `chat`) and the web UI load it automatically:

```yaml
hooks: "{{ project_slug }}.harness.hooks:AuditHooks"   # "package.module:ClassName"
```

(From your own code you can still pass an instance directly:
`run(..., hooks=AuditHooks())`. The web composes your hook *with* its streaming
hook, so a guardrail you mount here also runs under `serve`.)

## Load standalone extension modules

Every registry above (`@registry.tool` / `@register_paradigm` /
`@register_condition` / `@register_strategy`{% if spec.memory.enabled %} /
`@register_memory`{% endif %}) only runs when its module is imported. Editing a built-in file
(`tools.py`, `context.py`{% if spec.memory.enabled %}, `memory.py`{% endif %}) is imported for free. To keep
your extensions in a **separate module** instead, list it in `config.yaml` so it's
imported at startup:

```yaml
extensions:
  - {{ project_slug }}.ext.my_tools      # any importable module(s); imported before the loop runs
```

The CLI, `serve`, and `{{ project_slug }} info` import these first, so your
registrations show up in `info`{% if interfaces.web %} / `GET /registries`{% endif %} and are selectable by name. A
config name that still doesn't resolve is reported: hard errors for a role's
profile / a paradigm / the context strategy (fail-fast), and a warning for a
context condition{% if spec.memory.enabled %} / memory backend{% endif %} that isn't registered (skipped at runtime —
likely a typo or a module you forgot to list here).

## Add a paradigm

A *paradigm* is one way to run a turn. The built-ins live in
`src/{{ project_slug }}/harness/paradigms/` — `agent` (default){% for p in paradigms %}{% if p == "plan" %}, `plan`{% elif p == "ask" %}, `ask`{% endif %}{% endfor %}.
Each is a self-contained file with its own loop; **they never import each other**,
so editing one can't affect the others. Adding your own is the same shape as
adding a tool — write a function, register it, then enable it in `config.yaml`:

```python
# src/{{ project_slug }}/harness/paradigms/mymode.py
from . import RunResult, register_paradigm
from ..trace import Trace

@register_paradigm("mymode")
def run(user_input, *, config, trace=None, **kwargs):
    # accept **kwargs (client/client_for/registry/hooks/on_delta/role) and use
    # what you need; the easiest start is to copy agent.py and tweak its loop.
    t = trace or Trace(enabled=False)
    return RunResult(answer=f"echo: {user_input}", steps=0, stop_reason="final",
                     trace=t, mode="mymode")
```

Import it so it registers (add `from . import mymode` at the bottom of
`paradigms/__init__.py`), then list it in `config.yaml`:

```yaml
paradigms:
  enabled: [agent, mymode]   # add your paradigm's name here
  default: agent
```

Now `{{ project_slug }} run --mode mymode "..."` (or the web dropdown) routes to
it. Once imported, the registered paradigm appears in `{{ project_slug }} info`{% if interfaces.web %} and
the web Paradigms tab (tick it to enable; it then joins the chat-mode dropdown){% endif %} —
and an enabled name that isn't registered is flagged. This is a written-out
name→function dispatch over a fixed registry — **not** a runtime "paradigm
engine" / dynamic graph / DSL. To make a read-only paradigm,
filter tools with `registry.active_names(config.enabled_tool_names(),
allow_high_risk=False)` (that's exactly how `plan`/`ask` stay safe).

To make your paradigm resumable (`run --continue` / `--resume`, `chat`, the web
session), accept `history` (prior messages, no system prompt) and seed your loop
with `[system, *history, user]`, then return the conversation body as
`RunResult.messages` — copy how `agent.py` does it. Ignore both and the paradigm
still works, it just won't resume. Saving/loading lives in `harness/session.py`
(plain JSON under `.harness/sessions/`, message text only — never secrets).

## Stop / continue / re-ask

A turn can be **stopped** mid-flight — Ctrl-C in the CLI, the Stop button in the
web chat. Stop is cooperative: a `cancel` predicate threads from the entry point
into the paradigm and down into `client.stream`, which closes the HTTP stream
mid-token. The loop only stops at a **clean boundary** — the model output is
dropped (never half-appended) or a whole tool batch finishes first — so the saved
`messages` are always a legal sequence (it never leaves an assistant `tool_calls`
without its results). A stopped turn ends with `stop_reason="interrupted"`.

> Stop ends the model + the loop. It does **not** roll back side effects already
> done (files written, requests sent) — that's what your git / Docker isolation is
> for. The harness only governs the conversation, not the world.

**Continue** is automatic: a stopped (or crashed) turn saves its partial body, so
the next message — `run --continue`, `chat`, or just sending again in the web —
resumes with the full context. The model sees the original question and whatever
tool steps already ran, so it picks up where it left off. There's no separate
"continue" command; continuing is just the next message on the same session.

Crash safety: each completed step is **write-ahead checkpointed** (an atomic
single-file write, `status="running"`), so even a `kill -9` mid-turn leaves a
resumable session. On load, a session that wasn't cleanly finished is checked for
a dangling `tool_use` (an assistant tool call with no result, only possible from a
hard crash mid-batch) and a synthetic error result is injected so the resumed
history is legal — see `repair_orphan_tool_results` in `harness/session.py`.
{% if interfaces.web %}
**Re-ask** (web only): click ✎ on any earlier question to edit it, confirm in-page
("discards the conversation after this point"), and the chat regenerates from that
turn. This is a **destructive truncation** — the later messages are overwritten,
not forked (`session.truncate_to_turn` + `?edit_turn=N` on `/chat`). The CLI has
no re-ask (it would need a full rewind TUI); edit the session JSON by hand instead.
{% endif %}

### Self-correction & reflection (do it with a real signal)

The `agent` loop already self-corrects *online*: a failing tool comes back as an
`ERROR: ...` observation and the model adapts on the next step. That covers the
common case without any extra mode.

For deliberate *post-hoc* reflection (Reflexion: critique → retry), gate it on a
**real success signal** — run your tests / a validator and only loop back on
failure — rather than asking the model to grade its own work blind (an
unconditional self-critique mostly burns tokens and can't see its own blind
spots). The clean way is your own paradigm that wraps `agent.run`:

```python
# critique-and-retry ONLY while an external check fails
@register_paradigm("reflexion")
def run(user_input, *, config, **kwargs):
    from .agent import run as agent_run
    result = agent_run(user_input, config=config, **kwargs)
    for _ in range(2):                       # bounded retries
        if check_passes(result.answer):      # YOUR signal: tests, schema, lint...
            break
        result = agent_run(
            f"{user_input}\n\nYour previous answer failed: {why_it_failed}. Fix it.",
            config=config, **kwargs,
        )
    return result
```

(Importing `agent.run` here is fine — that's reusing the base loop, not two
built-in paradigms importing each other.)

## Per-LLM cost limits & tracing

Spend is tracked **per LLM profile** in a small persistent ledger
(`harness/usage.py` → `.harness/usage.json`) that accumulates token usage across
runs. It covers the two LLM calls that go through `Trace.add_usage`: the main
generation loop and context compaction (each attributed to whichever profile made
it — generation and compaction count separately when they use different
profiles). It never holds secrets — only profile names and token counts.

Cost is *derived* from each profile's per-1M prices in `config.yaml`, so editing a
price re-prices the whole history at once:

```yaml
llms:
  - name: default
    model: gpt-4o-mini
    input_cost_per_million: 0.15      # price / 1M tokens, your currency
    output_cost_per_million: 0.60
    cached_input_cost_per_million: 0.0   # 0 = bill cached prompt tokens at the input price
    cost_limit: 5.00                  # 0 = no limit
```

When a profile's accrued cost reaches its `cost_limit`, the loop **stops using
that LLM**: it refuses the next call to it and ends the turn with
`stop_reason="llm_budget"` and a notice naming the LLM, instead of spending more.
Set prices for the limit to mean anything (with all prices `0` cost stays `0` and
the limit never fires). Usage is keyed by profile **name** and only resets when
you clear it by hand — renaming a profile starts a fresh counter; changing its
model keeps accumulating under the same name.

Manage it all on the web **Budget** page (per-LLM prices, limit, accrued cost,
and a Clear button) or from the CLI:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} usage              # show per-LLM accrued cost + limits
{{ project_slug }} usage clear -n default   # reset one LLM (omit -n for all)
```

Every run also writes a JSONL trace under the configured `trace_dir` with per-step
token counts and cost. Inspect a run with `cat traces/run-*.jsonl`.

For troubleshooting there is also an opt-in **local debug log**
(`observability.debug: true`, written to `<debug_dir>/debug.log`, toggleable
live from the web /config Observability page). It records the loop / tool /
LLM / config lifecycle as names, counts, durations and error types — **never**
message content, tool arguments, tool results, or secrets — so it is safe to
share when reporting a problem. It stays on this machine (`logs/` is
gitignored); nothing is ever uploaded. See `harness/debuglog.py`.

## Tune the context window

`harness/context.py` keeps the history within budget before each LLM call. It
splits cleanly into *when* and *how*, each a thin name→function registry (same
shape as tools / paradigms — a written-out dispatch, not an engine):

```yaml
context:
  strategy: summarize     # how: truncate | summarize | none (or your own)
  keep_last_turns: 6       # how many recent turns the strategy keeps
  combine: or              # when: or = any trigger fires; and = all must fire
  max_tool_result_chars: 100000  # cap one tool result before it enters history (0 = off)
  triggers:                # when: condition name -> threshold
    window_pct: 0.85       # compact at 85% of the profile's context_window
    # max_tokens: 192000   # ...and/or an absolute token count (real usage when known)
    # max_turns: 40        # ...and/or after this many user turns
```

Compaction is **usage-driven**: the real `prompt_tokens` the provider reported on
the previous response feeds the trigger (a char estimate is only a first-call
fallback). Built-in conditions are `window_pct` (real prompt tokens as a fraction
of the active profile's `context_window` — the default; **inactive until that
profile sets `context_window`**, so a large-window model is never compacted too
early), `max_tokens` (an absolute count), and `max_turns` (user turns); built-in
strategies are `truncate`, `summarize` (folds older turns via the `compaction`
role), and `none`. Empty `triggers` (or `strategy: none`) means it never fires.
`max_tool_result_chars` is the first gate — it caps a single huge tool result
(with a `[truncated N chars]` marker) before it can blow the window. If a request
still overflows, the loop force-compacts once and retries that step automatically.

Add your own the same way you add a tool — write a function, register it, then
name it in `config.yaml`. A condition may take an optional third `info` argument
(`info.last_prompt_tokens` / `info.context_window`) for usage-driven logic; the
older two-arg `(messages, threshold)` form still works unchanged:

```python
from {{ project_slug }}.harness.context import register_condition, register_strategy

@register_condition("max_chars")        # when: (messages, threshold[, info]) -> bool
def max_chars(messages, threshold):
    return sum(len(m.get("content") or "") for m in messages) > threshold

@register_strategy("head_tail")         # how: rewrite messages to fit
def head_tail(messages, ctx, *, summarizer=None):
    system = [m for m in messages if m.get("role") == "system"]
    return system + messages[-2 * ctx.keep_last_turns:]
```

```yaml
context:
  strategy: head_tail
  combine: or
  triggers:
    max_chars: 600000
```

This is a runtime knob — change the strategy, the conditions, or their
thresholds without regenerating. Once your module is imported, the registered
strategy/condition shows up in `{{ project_slug }} info`{% if interfaces.web %} and the web Context
tab's dropdowns{% endif %} — and a config name that isn't registered is flagged there.
{% if interfaces.web %}
## Web interface

`interfaces/web.py` is a thin FastAPI app (`{{ project_slug }} serve`):

- `GET /chat?message=...&stream=true` — Server-Sent Events. With `stream=true`
  (default) the assistant's answer is streamed **token-by-token** (`event: token`)
  for a live typing effect; tool calls/results and a `final` event (answer +
  token/cost stats) are always emitted. The loop runs in a worker thread and
  pushes events onto a queue. Token streaming rides on `loop.run(on_delta=...)`,
  which calls the client's `stream()` instead of `complete()`. Set `stream=false`
  for a single non-streamed response.
- `GET|POST /config` — read/edit the **runtime behavioral** config (the same
  fields as `config.yaml`). A POST applies in-process AND writes the edited
  fields back to `config.yaml` (comments preserved via ruamel.yaml), so it
  survives a restart. Secrets are never returned or editable — profiles only
  carry env-var names, so only NAMES are ever written to the file.

The CLI streams too: `{{ project_slug }} run --stream "..."`. To stream from your
own code, pass `on_delta` (a `Callable[[str], None]`) to `run()`.

To add a structural change (a new endpoint, a new module), edit `web.py`
directly — it's yours. Behavioral knobs belong in `config.yaml` / the panel.{% endif %}{% if spec.mcp.enabled %}
## MCP tools (the capability baseline)

This harness ships no built-in productivity tools — its real capabilities come
from [MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) servers. `harness/mcp.py` connects
servers and registers their tools into the same registry as the built-ins, so
the loop is untouched. `config.yaml` is prefilled with a baseline:

- **`fetch`** (`uvx mcp-server-fetch`) — fetch a known URL as markdown; **on**.
- **`ddg-search`** (`uvx duckduckgo-mcp-server`) — keyless web **search** +
  `fetch_content`; **on** by default (gives the agent real "search → read" research).
- **`git`** (`uvx mcp-server-git`) — read tools (`git_status`/`git_diff`/`git_log`/
  …) **on**; mutating tools (`git_commit`/`git_add`/…) **off**.
- **`desktop-commander`** (`npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander`) — terminal +
  filesystem; predefined but **all off** (high-risk). Needs Node. Flip a tool to
  `true` to enable.

The system prompt tells the model which capabilities are installed-but-disabled
(and how to enable them), so a turned-off server like Desktop Commander is never
*silently* missing — the agent will ask you to enable it when a task needs it.

A discovered tool is offered to the model **only** when allowlisted in `tools:`
as `<server>__<tool>`. Per-tool risk: a name listed in the server's `safe_tools`
registers as `risk="safe"` (read-only — also offered to the `plan`/`ask`
paradigms); everything else is `risk="high"` (mutating; agent-only). A server is
launched **only when at least one of its tools is enabled**, so the off-by-default
Desktop Commander stays dormant until you opt in. Secrets (`env` / `auth_env`)
are referenced by env-var **name** and resolved from `.env` at runtime.
{% if interfaces.web %}
You don't have to write the `<server>__<tool>` lines by hand: in the web **Config
→ Tools** tab, hit **Scan MCP** (it runs on first open too) — each configured
server's tools are listed under a collapsed group with a **master checkbox**
(toggle the whole server's tools at once); tick one to add it to the allowlist and
Save.

The **Config → MCP** tab is the management view: each server shows a live
connection dot — **green** = connected, **amber** = connecting (e.g. a `uvx`/`npx`
package still cold-downloading; the page re-polls until it settles), **red** =
unreachable (with the error) — plus its transport + tool counts and editable
fields. Each server has its own **Reconnect** button (retry just that one without
disturbing the others); **Reconnect all** rebuilds them together. Add / edit /
delete a server there and it **reconnects immediately** (writing `config.yaml`
back, env-var names only). This lets the product spawn local commands / reach URLs
you configure — a **local, trusted control**, so don't expose `/config` or `/mcp`
on a public network.
{% endif %}
On the CLI, `{{ project_slug }} mcp status` health-checks every configured server
(connectivity + tool counts; an unreachable server is shown with its error).

Remote servers use `url` (+ optional `auth_env`). Set `transport: sse` for an
older server that speaks the legacy HTTP+SSE transport instead of Streamable HTTP
(the `http` default); stdio servers infer their transport from `command`.

```yaml
mcp:
  servers:
    - name: git
      command: uvx
      args: ["mcp-server-git"]   # no --repository pin: the agent passes repo_path per call, so the server stays up in any cwd
      safe_tools: ["git_status", "git_log", "git_diff"]   # read-only -> risk=safe
    - name: remote-example
      url: https://mcp.example.com/sse
      transport: sse          # legacy HTTP+SSE; omit (or `http`) for Streamable HTTP
      auth_env: EXAMPLE_TOKEN  # env-var NAME holding the bearer token
  connect_timeout_seconds: 120  # generous; first uvx/npx cold starts can be slow on Windows / behind the GFW
  call_timeout_seconds: 120     # per tool CALL: a hung server fails this one call instead of wedging the turn
  # proxy / npm_registry / pip_index: optional overrides (see "Network" below)
tools:
  - name: git__*                 # wildcard: enable EVERY tool of this server
    enabled: true
  # Or list tools individually as <server>__<tool> to narrow the allowlist:
  # - name: git__git_status
  #   enabled: true
  # - name: git__git_commit
  #   enabled: false             # mutating -> off until you opt in
```

A `<server>__*` wildcard enables the server's whole toolset (read + mutating),
including tools it adds later. Risk is still per-tool: tools in `safe_tools`
register as `risk=safe` (offered to plan/ask); the rest are `risk=high`
(agent-only). To narrow, replace the wildcard with explicit `<server>__<tool>`
lines (or toggle individual tools on the web Tools panel — a fully-on server
saves back as the wildcard).

### Network (proxy / registry) for npx & uvx servers

A stdio server launched with `npx`/`uvx` (e.g. desktop-commander) fetches its
package the first time it connects. The harness makes that work on any network
automatically: it reuses the **system proxy** and probes for a **reachable
registry** — the official one, else a domestic mirror — directly or through the
proxy, and injects the winner into the server's subprocess. Nothing to configure
for direct-internet, GFW, or corporate-proxy machines.

Pre-download up front so the first connect is instant (and an unreachable registry
is reported clearly instead of timing out): `{{ project_slug }} mcp warm`.

Override the auto-choice under `mcp:` when needed (values only, never secrets):

```yaml
mcp:
  proxy: "http://host:8080"                        # default: the system proxy
  npm_registry: "https://registry.npmmirror.com"   # npx/npm registry (default: auto)
  pip_index: "https://pypi.tuna.tsinghua.edu.cn/simple"  # uvx/uv index (default: auto)
```

(Auto-detection covers env vars + the Windows/macOS system proxy; a PAC-only
`AutoConfigURL` setup is not parsed — set `mcp.proxy` explicitly there.)

### Offline & Docker

uvx-based servers (fetch, ddg-search, git) download on first use, then run from
cache. The generator prewarms that cache (`harnessmith new` / `--prewarm`), and
the `Dockerfile` warms it at build time and sets `UV_OFFLINE=1`, so `docker run`
works with no network. Node-based servers (Desktop Commander) need Node on the
host and are not baked. Because Windows rarely ships Node, the one-click launcher
(`{{ launch_name }}.bat` / `.sh`) detects a missing `node`/`npx` and offers to
fetch a **portable Node** (pinned LTS) into your user folder — `%LOCALAPPDATA%\{{ project_slug }}`
on Windows, `~/.local/share/{{ project_slug }}` on macOS/Linux — and prepends it to
PATH for that run (no admin, nothing committed to the repo; an official or
npmmirror download, or skip). If you launch some other way, a missing Node is
reported with an install hint instead of a raw spawn error.

If a generated repo's first run *didn't* prewarm and `UV_OFFLINE` isn't set, a
`uvx`/`npx` server still downloads its package on first use (it just shows
**connecting** until it lands). Behind the GFW, when `pypi.org` / `registry.npmjs.org`
is unreachable, the child process is pointed at a domestic mirror automatically
(Tsinghua PyPI for `uvx`, npmmirror for `npx`) — but **only** if you haven't set
your own `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX` / `npm_config_registry`, so this is a no-op outside China.

### Windows

The harness core (uv, Python, Typer) is cross-platform, but a few MCP details
differ on native Windows:

- **Docker is the smoothest path** — the generated image is Linux, so `docker run`
  (via Docker Desktop / WSL2) sidesteps every Windows-specific quirk below.
- **`fetch` on native Windows** may need `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8` (a known
  mcp-server-fetch issue); set it in your environment if `fetch` times out.
- **Shell commands are OS-specific.** The system prompt tells the model the host
  OS so it writes PowerShell/cmd (not bash) when a shell tool is enabled; Desktop
  Commander also has a `defaultShell` setting.

### More servers (marketplace)

Add any server with `harnessmith new --mcp-server <name>` from the catalog, or
paste a block from a marketplace (Smithery / Glama / MCP.so / the official MCP
Registry) straight into `config.yaml` — the format is the same. SaaS catalogs
(e.g. Composio) are reached as **remote** servers (`url` + `auth_env`), no extra
package. The `mcp` dependency exists only because this repo was generated with
MCP enabled.{% endif %}{% if spec.skills.enabled %}
## Add a Skill

This harness supports the open [Agent Skills](https://modelcontextprotocol.io)
standard (`SKILL.md` + progressive disclosure) — `harness/skills.py`. The same
skill works with Claude, Cursor, and other Agent Skills tools. Drop a folder
under `skills/` (see `config.yaml` → `skills.dirs`):

```
skills/
  commit-helper/
    SKILL.md          # frontmatter: name + description (+ optional scripts/, references/)
```

```markdown
---
name: commit-helper
description: Write a conventional-commit message from a staged git diff. Use when asked to commit.
---

# Commit helper

1. Read the staged diff (e.g. the git MCP server's git_diff_staged).
2. Write a one-line conventional-commit subject, then a short body.
```

**Progressive disclosure:** only each skill's `name` + `description` is injected
into the system prompt (~100 tokens). When a skill is relevant the model loads
its full `SKILL.md` on demand with the `read_skill` tool (low-risk, allowlisted)
or any file tool. Heavier skills ship `scripts/` (run with a shell tool) and
`references/`/`assets/` (read with a file tool) — scripts are high-risk, so the
tool stays off until you enable it. Set `disable-model-invocation: true` in the
frontmatter to keep a skill out of the auto-prompt; reach it manually with
`{{ project_slug }} run "/skill-name ..."`.{% endif %}{% if spec.memory.enabled %}

## Long-term memory (cross-session)

`harness/memory.py` gives the agent a small **self-maintained note that persists
across sessions** — durable facts, user preferences, decisions — injected into
every system prompt. It's tool-driven: the agent decides what's worth keeping and
writes it with the `memory_append` / `memory_write` tools (and reads it back with
`memory_read`). The built-in `file` backend keeps one human-readable markdown note
(`config.yaml` → `memory.path`, default `.harness/memory.md`, gitignored, **never
holds secrets**). Read it, edit it, or delete the file to forget. Manage it by
hand with `{{ project_slug }} memory show | clear | path`{% if interfaces.web %} or the web `GET/POST/DELETE
/memory` endpoints{% endif %}.

Memory is a few notes the agent curates **on purpose** — distinct from RAG
(external-corpus retrieval, not built here). Keep it small: only
`inject_max_chars` of the note is injected (most-recent slice), so it stays thin.

```yaml
memory:
  backend: file
  path: .harness/memory.md
  inject_max_chars: 4000   # cap on injected note size (also the "oversized" mark)
  read_only: false         # true = only the read tool (the agent can't write memory)
  policy: null             # optional instruction (what/when to remember), injected with memory
  auto_consolidate: false  # condense an oversized note at chat-session end (memory role)
```

`policy` shapes HOW the agent curates memory (e.g. "remember durable preferences
and decisions; skip transient chit-chat") without editing the main system prompt —
it's injected alongside the memory section every turn.

### Keeping a growing note small (consolidation)

The note grows as the agent appends; once it passes `inject_max_chars` it is
**oversized** — older notes stop being recalled (only the most-recent slice is
injected). *Consolidation* rewrites it smaller with the **`memory`-role** model (the
"memory manager" — bind it under `roles:` to a capable model, or leave it unset to
reuse the first profile; keep durable facts, merge duplicates, drop stale).
Following how memory frameworks (Mem0 / Letta / LangMem) do it, this runs in the
**background** — at a session boundary or on demand — **never in the reply hot
path**, so a quick reply or a weak generation model is never disturbed:

- **Manual**: `{{ project_slug }} memory consolidate`{% if interfaces.web %}, or the web Memory panel's "Consolidate" button (`POST /memory/consolidate`){% endif %}.
- **Automatic**: set `auto_consolidate: true` — the CLI `chat` consolidates an
  oversized note when the session ends (otherwise it just prints a nudge).{% if interfaces.web %} The web
  Memory panel also surfaces the note, its size, an oversized warning, and the
  read-only / capacity / policy / backend knobs.{% endif %}

### Write your own backend

A *backend* decides where memory lives and what's recalled/recorded — the same
name→thing dispatch as tools / paradigms / context (not an engine). Subclass
`MemoryBackend`, override what you need, register it, and point `memory.backend`
at its name. The contract is two core methods plus three optional lifecycle hooks
(default no-ops — "override to opt in"):

```python
from {{ project_slug }}.harness.memory import MemoryBackend, register_memory
from {{ project_slug }}.harness.tools import Tool, SAFE

@register_memory("vecdb")
class VecMemory(MemoryBackend):
    def recall(self, query=""):           # text injected into the system prompt
        return my_vector_search(query)
    def record(self, messages):           # persist a completed turn (optional)
        my_store.add(messages)
    def tools(self):                       # optional: expose search/store tools
        return [Tool(name="memory_search", description="...", parameters={...},
                     func=my_search, risk=SAFE)]
    # optional: on_session_end(messages)  -> a conversation ended (chat exit)
    # optional: on_compact(dropped)       -> rescue insight before older turns drop
```

```yaml
memory:
  backend: vecdb        # your registered name
```

Import your module so `@register_memory` runs; it then shows in
`{{ project_slug }} info`{% if interfaces.web %} and `GET /registries`{% endif %}, and an unknown
`backend:` name is reported (and simply injects nothing) rather than crashing.
This is a thin own-code registry — **no** framework, no cloud memory service.{% endif %}
